FLOORFORGE_44 — a seventh free tool: the moisture & readiness log.

WHAT IT IS

Every other tool here helps a contractor win work and document it. This one is
the only one that can stop the job — and the only one that produces evidence on
a day when nothing appears to be wrong.

Moisture is the dominant cause of hardwood finish failure: cupping, crowning,
gaps, delamination, adhesive failure. It is also the argument a contractor has
to win months later when a homeowner points at a cupped board. A reading taken
on the day, written down, against a published limit, with the document that
limit came from printed beside it, is the difference between a warranty claim
and a conversation. Nothing else in this niche gives that away free.

  /moisture     wood subfloor vs flooring moisture, by face width
                concrete slabs by ASTM F2170 or ASTM F1869
                jobsite temperature and humidity
                the number of slab test locations the standard wants
                a dated record, printable, saved to the job

WHOSE NUMBERS THESE ARE — THE POINT OF THE WHOLE PATCH

None of them are FloorForge's. Every threshold was researched against published
sources before a line of UI was written, and every one is rendered on screen and
in print next to the reading it judged:

  4% / 2%       NWFA Installation Guidelines — solid strip under 3 in.: the
                subfloor must be no more than 4% above the flooring; solid plank
                3 in. and wider: no more than 2%. The width is where the rule
                changes, which is exactly the part that gets missed.
  60–80 °F      NWFA — acclimate and install at normal living conditions,
  30–50% RH     30–50% relative humidity for most areas.
  75% RH        ASTM F2170 — in-situ relative humidity at or below 75%.
  3 lb          ASTM F1869 — calcium chloride emission at or below 3 lb per
                1,000 sq ft per 24 h.
  3 + 1/1000    ASTM F2170 — three test locations for the first 1,000 sq ft, one
                more per additional 1,000. EVERY location must pass; an average
                that clears the limit is not a pass, and the tool says so.

Sources are listed at the end of this file.

THREE JUDGEMENTS WORTH REVIEWING

1. FLOORING MOISTURE CONTENT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SCORED. There is no correct
   national number: acceptable MC depends on the equilibrium moisture content
   where the floor lives. The row renders, shows the reading, and says
   "Deliberately not scored — a pass/fail here would be invented", with the NWFA
   regional guidance beside it. Inventing a threshold would have been the easy
   thing and the dishonest one.

2. A SUBFLOOR DRIER THAN THE FLOORING IS A NOTE, NOT A FAILURE. NWFA's rule is
   directional — the subfloor must not be more than N% ABOVE the flooring. A dry
   subfloor does not violate that sentence, but the flooring will give up
   moisture and shrink, so it surfaces as "worth noting" with the consequence
   named rather than being silently passed or wrongly failed.

3. "CLEAR" NEVER MEANS "READY". The headline for a clean run is "Nothing over a
   published limit", and the subhead is "This is a record of what you measured,
   not a certification that the floor is ready." The tool certifies nothing,
   does not replace a test the standards require a qualified person to run, and
   states that the flooring manufacturer's specification governs where it
   differs.

HONESTY CONSTRAINTS

No FloorForge performance figure, customer count, testimonial, case study,
certification or partner logo appears. Nothing implies shipped hardware or an
existing customer — the tool works with no FloorForge hardware and says so.
verify44 asserts the absence of "guaranteed", "certified", "we recommend" and
"FloorForge standard" on the rendered page.

